    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2}+4 x y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,3).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} y^{2}+4 x y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} y^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x y) \\\\  & =2 y^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2})+4 y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2 y^{2}\\cdot (2 x)+4 y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =4 x y^{2}+4 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,3)} & =4 (1) (3)^{2}+4 (3) \\\\  & =36+(12) \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$",
